|> Exercise
    handle=_spherical_to_cartesian_coordinates_1_

    A point $P \in \rr^3$ at distance $R$ from the
    origin forms an angle $\phi$ with the positive
    $z$ axis, while $P$'s projection onto the $xy$-plane
    is at a counterclockwise angle $\theta$ from the
    positive $x$ axis:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch5_ex_spherical_coordinates_statement.svg

    What are $x$, $y$ and $z$ in terms of $R$,
    $\phi$ and $\theta$?

    |> Solution

        Since $\phi$ is an ordinary geometric angle
        between two lines, (as opposed to: an oriented
        angle, a.k.a., “signed angle”), we have

        $$
        0 \leq \phi \leq 2\eta
        $$

        (or

        $$
        0 \leq \phi \leq 180^\circ
        $$

        equivalently—and recall that 

        $$
        180^\circ = 2\eta
        $$

        _as real numbers_, so there absolutely no fudging
        going on if we write it the second way), 
        and
        $$
        \sin(\phi) \geq 0
        $$
        and
        $$
        R\sin(\phi) \geq 0
        $$
        and from which $R\sin(\phi)$, as it appears in the 
        following diagram, is the actual _length_ of
        $P$'s projection onto the $xy$-plane:
        
        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_spherical_coordinates_Rsin_phi.svg
        
        ...whereas $R\cos(\phi)$, as it appears above, is a

        __signed length__

        (or “coordinate”) (or “real number”, haha) as

        $$
        \cos(\phi)
        $$

        is negative for $\eta < \phi \leq 2\eta$, positive
        for $0 \leq \phi < \eta$! But in any case that is what
        we want, since $z$ switches from positive to negative
        at $\phi = \eta$ just like $\cos(\phi)$,
        giving us

        $$
        z = R\cos(\phi)
        $$

        and “one coordinate down”.
       
        For $x$ and $y$, then, we are left in a “classical”
        situation of converting polar coordinates to Cartesian
        coordinates in the $xy$-plane; only  the “$r$” of
        polar coordinates is now “$R\sin(\phi)$” (but
        which is nonnegative, happily, as noted):
        
        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_spherical_coordinates_xy_plane.svg
        
        Thus:
        
        |> Boxed
            style=margin-bottom:-5px;

            $$
            x = R\sin(\phi)\cos(\theta)
            $$

            $$
            y = R\sin(\phi)\sin(\theta)
            $$

            $$
            z = R\cos(\phi)
            $$
        
        |> StarDivider
